@style(../styles/IDocDemo.style)
@title(Octo 10: descriptive statistics and distributions)

All data here is invented. Random draws are seeded, so a reader sees the same numbers every time.

@section(Centre and spread)

Each of these summarises a sample in one number; with a matrix they work down columns.

@octo
x = [12 15 11 18 14 15 22 13 15 16];
mean(x), median(x), mode(x)
std(x), var(x), range(x), iqr(x), mad(x)
min(x), max(x), sum(x)
prctile(x, [25 50 75]), quantile(x, 0.9)
skewness(x), kurtosis(x), moment(x, 3)
zscore(x)(1:3)
normalize(x, "range")(1:3)
@end

@section(Two variables)

Covariance and correlation measure how two variables move together.

@octo
h = [150 160 165 170 180]; w = [50 58 63 66 77];
cov(h, w), corr(h', w'), corrcoef(h, w)
@end

@section(Counting)

`tabulate` and `histc` count values; `crosstab` counts pairs.

@octo
grade = [1 2 2 3 3 3 1 2];
tabulate(grade)
histc(grade, 1:3)
crosstab([1 1 2 2], [1 2 1 2])
@end

@section(Distribution functions)

Each family has `pdf`, `cdf`, `inv` and `rnd` forms; the generic `pdf`/`cdf`/`icdf` take the family's name.

@octo
normpdf(0), normcdf(1.96), norminv(0.975)
binopdf(3, 10, 0.5), binocdf(3, 10, 0.5)
poisspdf(2, 3), poisscdf(2, 3)
exppdf(1, 2), expcdf(1, 2)
unifpdf(0.5, 0, 1), tcdf(2, 10), tinv(0.975, 10)
chi2cdf(3.84, 1), fcdf(4, 2, 10), gampdf(1, 2, 1), betapdf(0.5, 2, 2)
pdf("Normal", 0, 0, 1), cdf("Poisson", 2, 3), icdf("Normal", 0.5, 0, 1)
@end

@section(Random numbers and fitting)

Draw from a family, then fit the family back to the draws.

@octo
r = normrnd(10, 2, 1, 2000);
[mu, sigma] = normfit(r)
numel(random("Poisson", 4, 1, 5))
lam = mean(poissrnd(4, 1, 500))
@end

@section(Looking at a distribution)

`ksdensity` smooths a histogram; `ecdf` and `qqplot` compare a sample with a model.

@octo
r = randn(1, 300);
[f, xi] = ksdensity(r); plot(xi, f); title("kernel density");
figure; [F, xe] = ecdf(r); stairs(xe, F); title("empirical CDF");
figure; qqplot(r);
@end
